{"description":"The only command that acquires headers (bitcoin-time note §10). It reads the file, validates every header (linkage, proof of work, the exact retarget, median time past, future time against this clock, version rules) and persists the chain in this reader's state: the store's git directory, or COMMONS_STATE_DIR for a store that is not git-backed; never inside the store. A header that fails validation stops the sync with its reason; what validated before it is kept, and the sync does not count as completed (the view's last-sync time does not move). Running it again with headers already held changes nothing but the last-sync time. The tool ships no checkpoint. No network source is built: obtain the file from sources you choose. Read commands also re-check, against this chain, where an exact-bytes observation's pinned block is (its depth, or held off the accepted chain): only a `bip122:` referent naming this chain's genesis, and never its bytes, so the re-check moves the referent status and adds no corroboration. Other chains and source referents stay unchecked. Recommended: start from a bootstrap near the tip (--bootstrap-height at a recent retarget-period start, with the chainwork below it as you established it), not from genesis. Every read command re-validates the whole persisted chain when it loads it, so its cost grows with the headers held: a few thousand headers load in milliseconds, a chain synced from genesis (close to a million headers) costs every read on the order of a second and a few hundred MB. A bootstrap is a trust root you choose: anchors below it read unknown-height. Exit 0 the sync completed; 1 a header failed validation; 3 the sync did not complete for another reason (bounded, cancelled, no state directory, the database refused or locked); 2 did not start.","options":[{"option":"--from","summary":"concatenated raw 80-byte block headers, in chain order (required). Every header is validated; where the file came from grants nothing"},{"option":"--bootstrap-height","summary":"first sync only: the first 11 headers of the file are an explicit local trust root at this height (a retarget-period start), accepted without validation; every later header is validated on top of them. Without it the chain starts at genesis"},{"option":"--bootstrap-work","summary":"with --bootstrap-height: the cumulative chainwork below the bootstrap, in hex, as the reader established it"},{"option":"--max-headers","summary":"read at most this many headers in this sync (default 2000000); a sync that stops at the bound keeps what validated and does not count as a completed sync"}],"summary":"validate Bitcoin block headers from a file and persist them as this reader's chain view","usage":"commons anchor headers sync [--from <file>] [--bootstrap-height <n>] [--bootstrap-work <hex>] [--max-headers <n>] [--json]"}
{"description":"The covered head is the statement named (a line of your own log: an anchor bounds only its signer's log), else your log's one tip; a FORK in your log is refused until --prev names the line the anchors statement extends. The raw 32 bytes of the head's id are submitted to each calendar in turn; with at least one pending proof back, the proofs of every calendar that answered (each checked: it parses, roots at the digest submitted, carries a pending attestation) are merged into one proof holding every calendar's pending attestation, stored as a blob, and an anchors statement {covered, proof, proof_format: ots-detached-v1, services: the calendars that answered} is appended after a line whose history holds the head, the two as one journal record. A pending proof is a calendar's promise: readers show it pending, quality local, until an upgraded proof is held: run anchor upgrade once the calendars have put the commitment in a Bitcoin block (hours). Stamping the same head again appends another anchors statement; readers display the later one. Calendars are local policy: --calendar or $COMMONS_CALENDARS, no default list (the public OpenTimestamps calendars are examples, not defaults). Only https is dialled, with no proxy; non-global addresses are refused unless --allow-private; a calendar may redirect at most three times, only to https on its own host; a response over 10000 bytes is that calendar's failure. publish does not stamp: run anchor stamp after publish, commit, then push, which refuses a statement whose standing requires an anchor until an anchors line covers it. --dry-run prints the head, the digest, the calendars and the head's standing, and contacts nothing. Exit 0 stamped and committed (or the dry run; a calendar that failed beside one that answered is a warning); 1 refused after the check over your log (your key has a key event); 2 did not start (no calendar named, a calendar URL that is not https, the statement is not a line of your log, no usable key); 3 every calendar failed (nothing appended; the head's standing is printed: anchor required or anchor: none, and the rule that decided), a forked log without --prev, or calendars not available in this build.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--calendar","summary":"an OpenTimestamps calendar to submit to (https only; repeatable); else $COMMONS_CALENDARS (comma- or space-separated). There is no default list: calendars are your local policy"},{"option":"--timeout","summary":"give up on one calendar after this long (Go duration, e.g. 10s); default 10s"},{"option":"--allow-private","summary":"allow a calendar on a loopback, private, link-local, shared or other non-global address (a local calendar or mirror); refused by default, checked on the address actually dialled"}],"summary":"timestamp a line of your log: submit its id to OpenTimestamps calendars and commit the pending proof with an anchors statement","usage":"commons anchor stamp [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--calendar <url>]… [--timeout <duration>] [--allow-private] [<statement-id>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Reads the persisted chain view without changing it and without contacting anything: provider, validation mode, network and genesis, bootstrap identity, tip height, hash and chainwork, header count, last successful sync, freshness under the time policy (maximum view age, maximum tip age), the policy fingerprint, and the receipt files held. Prints no secret. Exit 0 a chain view is configured (fresh or not; freshness is a state it reports); 3 none is (no state directory, no database, or the database refused).","summary":"this reader's chain backend: provider, mode, network, bootstrap, tip, last sync, freshness, receipt count","usage":"commons anchor status [--json]"}
{"description":"Upgrades the anchors statement named (a line of your own log whose proof_format is ots-detached-v1), else every anchors line of your log that is the newest for its head and whose proof has no Bitcoin attestation yet. For each pending attestation in the proof whose calendar is in your calendar policy (--calendar or $COMMONS_CALENDARS; the URL matches with or without one trailing slash), the calendar is asked GET <calendar>/timestamp/<hex commitment>: 404 means still pending; a 200 answer (at most 10000 bytes) is parsed strictly and merged into the proof, keeping the pending attestations; an answer that does not root at the pending node is refused for that calendar only. A pending attestation naming a calendar outside your policy is listed as not in local calendar policy and not contacted. Only when the merged proof gains a Bitcoin attestation are the new proof blob and a new anchors statement {covered: the same head, proof, proof_format, services: the calendars that answered} appended after a line whose history holds the old anchor, all upgraded heads in one journal record. The old anchors line stays; readers verify the new proof against their synced headers (anchor headers sync). Only https is dialled, with no proxy; non-global addresses are refused unless --allow-private; a calendar may redirect at most three times, only to https on its own host. --dry-run lists the pending commitments and which calendar would be asked, and contacts nothing. Exit 0 upgraded at least one head, or every calendar asked said still pending (each head shows pending), or nothing needs upgrading; 1 refused after the check over your log (your key has a key event); 2 did not start (no calendar named, a calendar URL that is not https, the statement named is not an anchors line of your log or not ots-detached-v1, no usable key); 3 every calendar asked failed or no pending attestation names a calendar in your policy (nothing appended), the proof blob is not held, a forked log without --prev, or calendars not available in this build.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--calendar","summary":"an OpenTimestamps calendar you allow to be asked (https only; repeatable); else $COMMONS_CALENDARS. A pending attestation naming another calendar is reported and not contacted"},{"option":"--timeout","summary":"give up on one calendar request after this long (Go duration, e.g. 10s); default 10s"},{"option":"--allow-private","summary":"allow a calendar on a loopback, private, link-local, shared or other non-global address (a local calendar or mirror); refused by default, checked on the address actually dialled"}],"summary":"complete pending timestamps: ask the calendars for the Bitcoin path of each pending proof and commit an upgraded anchors statement","usage":"commons anchor upgrade [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--calendar <url>]… [--timeout <duration>] [--allow-private] [<anchors-statement>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Everything show prints, plus the statements behind it (hash, signer, status, log line, counted or not; the key events of their signers are among them), the policy digests (and the reader policy document), the evaluator and the time context. For a resource (its genesis id, an edition id, or an approval's id) it adds each edition's reasoning: the rule and predecessor it was evaluated under, and each approval counted or not. With --since <file>, an earlier explain --json of the same ref, it also prints what changed since then, each change with its cause: a statement added or removed (a key event named as one), a statement's status, log line or counted state, the hub, reader (each entry that differs) or time policy, the evaluator, or the time context; and whether the rest of the answer changed. Nothing is cached between runs: the file is the only memory. A file that is not an explain output of this ref is exit 2; otherwise the exit is explain's own.","options":[{"option":"--since","summary":"an earlier `explain --json` output of the same ref: print what changed since, each change with its cause (statement, key event, policy, evaluator, time context)"},{"option":"--policy","summary":"the reader policy (reader-policy-v1) to answer under (default: $COMMONS_POLICY; none when neither is set; nothing is read implicitly; format: docs/READER-POLICY.md)"}],"summary":"the statements, policy, evaluator and time context behind every token shown","usage":"commons explain [--since <file>] [--policy <file>] <digest|id> [--json]"}
{"description":"Each argument is a blob digest, or the id of a held statement whose content is fetched. A blob already held and intact is not fetched again; a corrupt held copy is replaced by a verified one (the corrupt file goes to quarantine). Only https locations are fetched, and http with --allow-http; content-addressed networks (ipfs:), object-store schemes and file: are not fetched: admit a local copy with pull. The transport uses no proxy. Exit 0 every blob is held and verified (a location that served wrong bytes before another served the right ones is reported, and still 0); 1 a blob was refused on a completed hash or size mismatch and no location served it; 3 a blob was not obtained without such a refusal (unreachable, no location named, a fetch limit, a scheme not fetched, no transport in this build, the statement not held); 2 did not start. A dry run exits 0 once the plan is made.","options":[{"option":"--allow-http","summary":"also fetch plain http locations (default: https only)"},{"option":"--allow-private","summary":"allow loopback, private, link-local, shared (100.64.0.0/10) and other non-global addresses (a local mirror); refused by default, checked on the address actually dialled"},{"option":"--idle-timeout","summary":"give up on a location that makes no progress for this long: no response headers, or no body bytes (Go duration, e.g. 30s, 5m); default 60s. Connecting and the TLS handshake keep a fixed 15s bound"},{"option":"--timeout","summary":"optional cap on one whole location, however much progress it makes (Go duration, e.g. 30m); default none (the idle timeout and max-bytes bound a transfer)"},{"option":"--max-bytes","summary":"the fetch limit max_fetch_bytes: a location whose declared size exceeds it is refused before dialling, and a stream with no declared size is cut off past it (default 1073741824)"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be fetched from where; dial nothing, install nothing (no fetch; no cache; no quarantine; no registry change)"}],"summary":"obtain blobs from the locations held `holds` statements name, verified by full hash; never publishes a holds","usage":"commons fetch [--allow-http] [--allow-private] [--idle-timeout <duration>] [--timeout <duration>] [--max-bytes <n>] [--dry-run] <digest|id>… [--json]"}
{"description":"Every fork of a held key log is listed under log_forks (FORK with its tips, or merged with the clearing line): informational, never a finding. Exit 0 no fatal finding (housekeeping findings are listed), 1 a fatal finding (corruption, an invalid statement, an unresolved reference a record promised, drift), 2 did not start (an unknown projection), 3 the check failed after it started.","options":[{"option":"--rebuild","summary":"drop and recompute this registered projection cold (none is registered by this version)"}],"summary":"check the store: blobs re-hashed, statements re-read, references resolved, journal and cache checked","usage":"commons fsck [--rebuild <projection>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Exit 0 the plan was made (dry run) or carried out; 2 did not start (an unusable hub-policy.json at the store root, a malformed duration or digest, --release with --apply or --grace, --key without --release, no usable key for --release, --release of a source profile the policy pins); 3 the operation failed after work started (the output says whether anything was committed).","options":[{"option":"--apply","summary":"delete the candidates (default: a dry run that deletes nothing)"},{"option":"--grace","summary":"the grace window for this run only (Go duration, e.g. 10m, 48h); default the policy window"},{"option":"--release","summary":"withdraw this store's serving holds for the blob and delete it, as one operation (signs; acts at once)"},{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file for --release (default: $COMMONS_KEY); a key file written by commons key new; never printed"}],"summary":"list (default) or delete unreferenced blobs outside the grace window; release a blob this store serves","usage":"commons gc [--apply] [--grace <duration>] [--release <digest>] [--key <file>] [--json]"}
{"description":"The store appends one line per operation it finishes (after the operation's statements are installed) and one per journal record it moves to quarantine/, under journal/: best-effort (a crash right after an operation may lose its line), bounded (8 MiB, then rotated to one older file), never transported, and read by nothing but this command, so deleting or editing it changes no other output. local_time is this machine's clock, evidence of nothing beyond it; replayed says the operation was finished by a later open or by repair rather than by the command that committed it. A line that does not parse is skipped and counted. Exit 0 always: with the history off (history off), with no history file yet, or with nothing matching, the list is empty and the output says why.","options":[{"option":"--limit","summary":"list at most n entries (default 50)"},{"option":"--op","summary":"only the entries of this operation id (64 lowercase hex)"}],"summary":"list operations this store finished, newest first (local, not authoritative)","usage":"commons history [--limit <n>] [--op <operation>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Exit 0 every object admitted (possibly labelled) or only control-plane paths reported, 1 at least one object refused, 2 the check did not start (not inside a hub, unknown layout, --base not a commit, unusable hub-policy.json at the base), 3 nothing refused and at least one object undecided, or git failed. Side effects: none (no registry change; no fetch; no cache).","options":[{"option":"--base","summary":"check the change between merge-base(<rev>, HEAD) and HEAD against the tree and hub-policy.json at <rev> (default: a full check of the tree at HEAD)"}],"summary":"check a change to the hub this directory is in, as the hub's CI does (writes nothing)","usage":"commons hub check [--base <rev>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Signs an identity_cut (rc/2) with the identity's root key. It cannot be undone: the delegate's acts under the grant after --last-good count toward nothing for the identity, now and for every reader that holds the cut; the acts at or before it keep counting. --last-good is required (an id, or none to keep nothing); the tool never picks it. It must be a line of the grant's delegate key that this store holds: any line of that key is accepted, a bare-key line (one signed without --as) included, because it only marks a prefix of the key's log, and each act kept must still be signed under this grant to count. A line of any other key is refused. Choosing it is yours alone: name the last act you can attest was the delegate's own, not the last act you have seen, because a thief's act inside the kept prefix keeps counting. Every held act under the grant is listed with what happens to it. --dry-run signs nothing and lists the same. Refused (exit 2) without --last-good or --reason; (exit 1) when your key is not the identity's root, the grant is not the identity's, or --last-good names a line of another key; exit 3 when the identity, the grant or the --last-good line is not held here. External signing (identity profile §8): --prepare <file> --signer <key> writes an external-signing package and appends nothing; sign its PAE bytes with the key elsewhere, then --complete <file> --signature <file|-> verifies the signature over the recomputed bytes, runs every check again, and commits only when your log has not moved since (else stale-prepare, exit 1).","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--prepare","summary":"do not sign: write an external-signing package (rc-signing-package/1) to this new file: the unsigned statement, the exact bytes to sign (PAE, base64), the signer, the proposed id, your log's head and a summary; needs --signer, takes no --key, appends nothing; an existing file is never overwritten"},{"option":"--signer","summary":"with --prepare: the public form of the key that will sign (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); the algorithm follows from the form"},{"option":"--complete","summary":"commit a package written by --prepare, with --signature: the bytes signed are recomputed from the package, the signature verified, every check of direct signing run again; refused, nothing appended, when your log moved since the package was prepared (stale-prepare)"},{"option":"--signature","summary":"with --complete: the signature over the package's PAE bytes in the statement encoding (ed25519: 128 lowercase hex; eip191-secp256k1: 0x + 130 lowercase hex); - reads standard input"},{"option":"--identity","summary":"the identity (its inception id); your key must be its root"},{"option":"--grant","summary":"the grant to cut (its statement id)"},{"option":"--last-good","summary":"required: a line of the delegate's key you vouch for, held here (any line of that key, bare-key lines included, not only an act under the grant; it and its ancestors keep counting), or none to keep nothing"},{"option":"--reason","summary":"required: why (a token, e.g. key_compromise, superseded)"}],"summary":"cut a grant: the delegate's acts after --last-good stop counting for the identity","usage":"commons identity cut [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--prepare <file>] [--signer <key>] [--complete <file>] [--signature <file|->] [--identity <id>] [--grant <id>] [--last-good <id|none>] [--reason <token>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Signs an identity_grant (rc/2) with the identity's root key naming --delegate and its seq-0 line --delegate-inception, with the permission set --permissions (profile §3: a kind, its targets, and for authored and observed an amend mode true, false or own). Refused (exit 2) when the delegate is the root key, a permission is malformed, or this reader would not read the grant as valid; refused (exit 1) when your key is not the identity's root or the delegate-inception held here is not the delegate's seq-0 line; exit 3 when the identity is not held here. When the delegate-inception line is not held here the grant is signed with a warning: a reader needs that line to place the delegate's acts. The delegate acts with publish --as <identity> --grant <grant id>. External signing (identity profile §8): --prepare <file> --signer <key> writes an external-signing package and appends nothing; sign its PAE bytes with the key elsewhere, then --complete <file> --signature <file|-> verifies the signature over the recomputed bytes, runs every check again, and commits only when your log has not moved since (else stale-prepare, exit 1).","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--prepare","summary":"do not sign: write an external-signing package (rc-signing-package/1) to this new file: the unsigned statement, the exact bytes to sign (PAE, base64), the signer, the proposed id, your log's head and a summary; needs --signer, takes no --key, appends nothing; an existing file is never overwritten"},{"option":"--signer","summary":"with --prepare: the public form of the key that will sign (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); the algorithm follows from the form"},{"option":"--complete","summary":"commit a package written by --prepare, with --signature: the bytes signed are recomputed from the package, the signature verified, every check of direct signing run again; refused, nothing appended, when your log moved since the package was prepared (stale-prepare)"},{"option":"--signature","summary":"with --complete: the signature over the package's PAE bytes in the statement encoding (ed25519: 128 lowercase hex; eip191-secp256k1: 0x + 130 lowercase hex); - reads standard input"},{"option":"--identity","summary":"the identity (its inception id); your key must be its root"},{"option":"--delegate","summary":"the delegate key K (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); never the root key"},{"option":"--delegate-inception","summary":"D: the statement id of the delegate key's seq-0 line, which pins the grant to that key's log"},{"option":"--permissions","summary":"a JSON array of permissions, e.g. [{\"kind\":\"authored\",\"targets\":{\"any_content\":true},\"amend\":false}]; sorted and deduplicated by the writer, 1..64"}],"summary":"grant a delegate key the right to act for an identity","usage":"commons identity grant [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--prepare <file>] [--signer <key>] [--complete <file>] [--signature <file|->] [--identity <id>] [--delegate <key>] [--delegate-inception <id>] [--permissions <json>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Signs an identity_inception (rc/2, profile fixed-root-v1) with --key (or $COMMONS_KEY) as the first line of that key's log; the identity's id is the inception's statement id. The root key must be fresh: a key with any line in this store is refused (exit 1), since the identity's lineage is the root key's whole log. That check sees this store only, not any other: use a key generated for this purpose, one that has signed nothing anywhere (the tool cannot see other stores, and an old key's earlier lines would become part of the identity's lineage). Use the root key for administration only (identity grant, identity cut) and keep it offline: it is the identity's only administrator, and fixed-root-v1 has no recovery if it is lost or stolen. --dry-run prints the exact statement and signs nothing; with --ts the dry run's id is the id the real run signs. External signing (identity profile §8): --prepare <file> --signer <key> writes an external-signing package and appends nothing; sign its PAE bytes with the key elsewhere, then --complete <file> --signature <file|-> verifies the signature over the recomputed bytes, runs every check again, and commits only when your log has not moved since (else stale-prepare, exit 1).","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--prepare","summary":"do not sign: write an external-signing package (rc-signing-package/1) to this new file: the unsigned statement, the exact bytes to sign (PAE, base64), the signer, the proposed id, your log's head and a summary; needs --signer, takes no --key, appends nothing; an existing file is never overwritten"},{"option":"--signer","summary":"with --prepare: the public form of the key that will sign (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); the algorithm follows from the form"},{"option":"--complete","summary":"commit a package written by --prepare, with --signature: the bytes signed are recomputed from the package, the signature verified, every check of direct signing run again; refused, nothing appended, when your log moved since the package was prepared (stale-prepare)"},{"option":"--signature","summary":"with --complete: the signature over the package's PAE bytes in the statement encoding (ed25519: 128 lowercase hex; eip191-secp256k1: 0x + 130 lowercase hex); - reads standard input"}],"summary":"create a fixed-root identity: sign its inception with a fresh root key","usage":"commons identity new [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--prepare <file>] [--signer <key>] [--complete <file>] [--signature <file|->] [--json]"}
{"description":"Creates the directory if needed and the store layout inside it (marker rc-store, layout rc-store/1). A directory that already holds a store of this layout is left as it is and the command exits 0 saying so; a non-empty directory that is not a store is refused and nothing is written. Creates no key: the next step is commons key new --out <file>.","summary":"create a store in a directory (default: the working directory)","usage":"commons init [<dir>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Writes the key as a small JSON file (format rc-key/0) holding the 32-byte Ed25519 seed or the secp256k1 scalar, mode 0600. The tool keeps no copy and uses no default location: name the file with --key or COMMONS_KEY on every signing command. Back it up once, somewhere only you can read; never reuse a payment key as a Commons key (wire-format note §4.1). Seed-phrase derivation is not built.","options":[{"option":"--out","summary":"the key file to create; its directory must exist; an existing file is never overwritten"},{"option":"--alg","summary":"ed25519 (default) or eip191-secp256k1"}],"summary":"generate a signing key into a new file (mode 0600, never overwritten)","usage":"commons key new [--out <file>] [--alg <alg>] [--json]"}
{"description":"With a key file: its signer and algorithm. With --ledger --ledger-path <path> (no file): the address of a Ledger device's key at that path, read from the Ethereum app, with the app's name and version, the path and the device; --ledger-confirm also shows the address on the device and waits for you to confirm it there (a refusal on the device is exit 1, and no signer is printed). With --ledger the key is a Ledger device running the Ethereum app (tested on the Speculos emulator for Nano S Plus and Nano X, and drilled by hand on a physical Nano X), opened over USB on Linux (hidraw) or as an emulator (tcp:<host>:<port>); on other systems the USB connector is not built. --ledger-path names the key (m/44'/60'/<account>'/0/<index>, no default): use an account that never holds funds. Identity PAEs are typically 300 to 800 bytes; the Ethereum app 1.23 accepts about 7.6 KB (less when a byte is not printable). Finish or reject a review on the device before running another command: the emulator refuses other requests while one is pending (ledger-busy), but a physical device may answer them and leave the review unresponsive until it is restarted. Interrupting the command (Ctrl-C) while the device waits for you closes the device and exits 3 (ledger-interrupted) without dismissing the review: reject it on the device before signing again. --ledger and --key are exclusive.","options":[{"option":"--ledger","summary":"sign with a Ledger device running the Ethereum app (eip191-secp256k1 only) instead of a key file; needs --ledger-path"},{"option":"--ledger-path","summary":"the BIP-32 path of the device's key, m/44'/60'/<account>'/0/<index> (' or h marks hardened); required with --ledger, no default. Use an account that never holds funds: never reuse a payment key as a Commons key"},{"option":"--ledger-device","summary":"which device: a hidraw node (/dev/hidrawN, Linux) or an emulator's APDU port (tcp:<host>:<port>, no hardware); else $COMMONS_LEDGER_DEVICE, else the one Ledger attached by USB"},{"option":"--ledger-timeout","summary":"how long to wait for you to approve or reject on the device (a Go duration such as 90s or 10m; 1s to 1h); default 10m. Every other exchange with the device waits 10s"},{"option":"--ledger-confirm","summary":"show the address on the device and wait for you to confirm it there (recommended once, before an identity's inception)"}],"summary":"print the signer and algorithm of a key file (never the key), or of a Ledger device's key","usage":"commons key show [--ledger] [--ledger-path <path>] [--ledger-device <device>] [--ledger-timeout <duration>] [--ledger-confirm] [<file>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Signs, with a key file, the PAE bytes recomputed from a package written by --prepare, on a machine that holds no store: the package is checked as package show checks it, the key must be the package's signer, the signature is made by the code the writer signs with and verified over the recomputed bytes before it is written, in the statement encoding --complete --signature reads (ed25519: 128 lowercase hex; eip191-secp256k1: 0x + 130 lowercase hex), newline-terminated. Every refusal writes nothing. There is no prompt: review the summary with package show (or --dry-run) first. It signs only the PAE of a statement the tool would prepare, never bytes it is given. --expect-pae-sha256 <hex> refuses, exit 1 and nothing signed or written, unless the sha256 of the PAE bytes is that digest. Carry the signature back and run the prepared command with --complete <package> --signature <file|->. Like package show it adds an advisory, and still signs, when the envelope's ts is more than 300 s ahead of this machine's clock. With --ledger the key is a Ledger device running the Ethereum app (tested on the Speculos emulator for Nano S Plus and Nano X, and drilled by hand on a physical Nano X), opened over USB on Linux (hidraw) or as an emulator (tcp:<host>:<port>); on other systems the USB connector is not built. --ledger-path names the key (m/44'/60'/<account>'/0/<index>, no default): use an account that never holds funds. Identity PAEs are typically 300 to 800 bytes; the Ethereum app 1.23 accepts about 7.6 KB (less when a byte is not printable). Finish or reject a review on the device before running another command: the emulator refuses other requests while one is pending (ledger-busy), but a physical device may answer them and leave the review unresponsive until it is restarted. Interrupting the command (Ctrl-C) while the device waits for you closes the device and exits 3 (ledger-interrupted) without dismissing the review: reject it on the device before signing again. --ledger and --key are exclusive. With --ledger the package must be for eip191-secp256k1, the open app must be the Ethereum app (else exit 3, naming the open app), and the device's address at the path must be the package's signer (else exit 2); before the device is asked, standard error shows what it will display: the PAE's length, that it is shown as text starting \"DSSEv1 \", and the EIP-191 message hash the device shows when its \"display hash\" setting is on. You approve on the device; the signature is verified as every reader verifies it before it is written. A refusal on the device is exit 1; a locked device, a closed app, a review already pending on the device, a message too long for the app, a refused request, a disconnect or a timeout (--ledger-timeout, default 10m) is exit 3. Every one writes nothing. --dry-run with --ledger checks the package, the app and the address and asks for no signature. The Ledger connector is the only hardware or wallet connector; --complete, identity and publish never open a device.","options":[{"option":"--package","summary":"the package written by --prepare; checked exactly as package show checks it, and refused, nothing written, when any derived member differs"},{"option":"--key","summary":"the key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); its signer must be the package's signer"},{"option":"--ledger","summary":"sign with a Ledger device running the Ethereum app (eip191-secp256k1 only) instead of a key file; needs --ledger-path"},{"option":"--ledger-path","summary":"the BIP-32 path of the device's key, m/44'/60'/<account>'/0/<index> (' or h marks hardened); required with --ledger, no default. Use an account that never holds funds: never reuse a payment key as a Commons key"},{"option":"--ledger-device","summary":"which device: a hidraw node (/dev/hidrawN, Linux) or an emulator's APDU port (tcp:<host>:<port>, no hardware); else $COMMONS_LEDGER_DEVICE, else the one Ledger attached by USB"},{"option":"--ledger-timeout","summary":"how long to wait for you to approve or reject on the device (a Go duration such as 90s or 10m; 1s to 1h); default 10m. Every other exchange with the device waits 10s"},{"option":"--out","summary":"the new file to write the signature to (never overwritten); - (the default) writes the signature line to standard output and the summary to standard error"},{"option":"--expect-pae-sha256","summary":"the sha256 of the PAE bytes (64 lowercase hex) that --prepare printed, carried by a separate channel: a match prints \"pae sha256: matches expected\"; a different digest is exit 1 naming both, and nothing is written"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"check the package and the key and print what would be signed; sign nothing, write nothing"}],"summary":"sign an external-signing package's PAE bytes with a key file (reads no store)","usage":"commons key sign [--package <file>] [--key <file>] [--ledger] [--ledger-path <path>] [--ledger-device <device>] [--ledger-timeout <duration>] [--out <file|->] [--expect-pae-sha256 <hex>] [--dry-run] [--json]"}
{"description":"One line per blob a held authored or holds statement names (authorship, grade, disclosures), then one line per resource this store holds a genesis for: its id (the genesis id), its current edition (or FORK and the tips), and how many controllers the current authority has. show <resource id> prints the full resource view. Each open FORK of a held key log adds a log_forks row (key, seq, tips).","options":[{"option":"--policy","summary":"the reader policy (reader-policy-v1) to answer under (default: $COMMONS_POLICY; none when neither is set; nothing is read implicitly; format: docs/READER-POLICY.md)"}],"summary":"every blob a statement in this store names, and every resource, one line of tokens each","usage":"commons list [--policy <file>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Reads a package written by --prepare (rc-signing-package/1) and recomputes, from its envelope alone, everything the package derives from it: the PAE bytes, the statement id, the alg, the signer, the log head's shape and the summary, exactly as --complete does. Exit 0 when every derived member matches, printing the summary, the signer, the alg, the proposed id, pae_sha256 and pae_bytes; exit 1 naming the first member that differs (and listing every one); exit 2 when the file is unreadable or not a package. It reads no store, so the log head is not checked against one: --complete checks it. Compare its pae_sha256 with the one --prepare printed, carried by a separate channel. When the envelope's ts is more than 300 s ahead of this machine's clock it adds an advisory (the store refuses such a ts at --complete); the exit is unchanged. --pae-out <file> writes the PAE bytes themselves for a signer that is not this tool. --expect-pae-sha256 <hex> compares the sha256 of the PAE bytes with a digest you carried by a separate channel: a match prints \"pae sha256: matches expected\"; a different digest is exit 1 naming both, and --pae-out then writes nothing.","options":[{"option":"--pae-out","summary":"write the exact PAE bytes a signature covers (the decoded pae member, no newline) to this new file, for a signer that is not this tool; never overwritten; written only when every check passes"},{"option":"--expect-pae-sha256","summary":"the sha256 of the PAE bytes (64 lowercase hex) that --prepare printed, carried by a separate channel: a match prints \"pae sha256: matches expected\"; a different digest is exit 1 naming both, and nothing is written"}],"summary":"check an external-signing package and print what its signature would cover (reads no store)","usage":"commons package show [--pae-out <file>] [--expect-pae-sha256 <hex>] <file> [--json]"}
{"description":"Signs with --key (or $COMMONS_KEY) and commits through the store's journal: the blob and its statements land together or not at all. A draft is never stored unsigned. Your log's next seq and prev come from the statements this store holds; a FORK in your log is refused until you name --prev. --intended-for runs the named edition's rules first and refuses on fail. With availability withheld and content under 64 bytes it warns that short content may be enumerable against its hash (a heuristic; include a nonce) and proceeds; the warning is not recorded. A rotate_key draft is signed as written: none of `commons rotate`'s checks run (successor, key events already held, merged tips), so the way out after a compromise stays open; it warns and proceeds. With --as <identity> --grant <id> the statement acts for a fixed-root identity (rc/2, the actor in the signed envelope): it is refused, nothing signed, unless the identity evaluator over the statements this store holds plus this line reads the act eligible (exit 2 when it is denied, naming why; exit 3 when it cannot decide, e.g. the grant or the identity is not held). A collection's genesis (a draft) may name typed controllers ({key: K} and/or {identity: I}): it is then signed rc/2 (body marker {}, no actor) from a bare key, and refused with nothing signed unless it reads valid (sorted and duplicate-free, a rule its controllers can satisfy). Controllers that are all plain keys stay rc/1. An edition naming an identity controller is refused (KNOWN-LIMITS L-21), and an act for an identity on an edition (--as) needs a grant carrying `edition` for a resource that already exists. A holds is written beside an authored or observed whose bytes this store keeps (--no-holds withholds it); it stays your key's own (rc/1): your key holds the bytes. External signing (identity profile §8): --prepare <file> --signer <key> writes an external-signing package and appends nothing; sign its PAE bytes with the key elsewhere, then --complete <file> --signature <file|-> verifies the signature over the recomputed bytes, runs every check again, and commits only when your log has not moved since (else stale-prepare, exit 1). Only an act (--as) is prepared; an authored act needs --no-holds, and its eligibility is read at --complete.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--draft","summary":"a JSON draft {\"kind\":…,\"body\":{…}}; the tool fills signer, seq, prev, ts and sig"},{"option":"--kind","summary":"authored, holds, describes or retracts (without --draft)"},{"option":"--content","summary":"the bytes the statement is about; installed in the store with the statements"},{"option":"--media-type","summary":"content.media_type"},{"option":"--type","summary":"authored type (default dataset)"},{"option":"--provenance","summary":"authored provenance object {workflow, inputs[, environment]}"},{"option":"--no-holds","summary":"authored or observed only: keep the bytes in the store but write no holds (a withheld artifact)"},{"option":"--about","summary":"describes: the blob or statement described"},{"option":"--note","summary":"describes: the note"},{"option":"--of","summary":"retracts: the statement retracted"},{"option":"--reason","summary":"retracts: the reason"},{"option":"--as","summary":"act for a fixed-root identity (its inception id): the statement is written rc/2 with x_actor_v1 {identity, grant}; needs --grant"},{"option":"--grant","summary":"with --as: the grant (its statement id) your key acts under"},{"option":"--amends","summary":"with --as, authored or observed only: the identity's earlier claims this one amends (identity profile §5)"},{"option":"--attribution","summary":"authored disclosure attribution"},{"option":"--availability","summary":"authored disclosure availability"},{"option":"--comparator","summary":"authored disclosure comparator"},{"option":"--description","summary":"authored disclosure description"},{"option":"--extends","summary":"authored disclosure extends"},{"option":"--fulfills","summary":"authored disclosure fulfills"},{"option":"--intended-for","summary":"run the edition's rules on the bytes before they are pinned (your own policy, chosen by naming the edition); fail refuses, ok or unchecked is recorded"},{"option":"--licence","summary":"authored disclosure licence"},{"option":"--licence-texts","summary":"authored disclosure licence_texts"},{"option":"--pre-registration","summary":"authored disclosure pre_registration"},{"option":"--replication","summary":"authored disclosure replication"},{"option":"--sampling","summary":"authored disclosure sampling"},{"option":"--shelf-life-days","summary":"authored disclosure shelf_life_days"},{"option":"--supersedes","summary":"authored disclosure supersedes"},{"option":"--prepare","summary":"do not sign: write an external-signing package (rc-signing-package/1) to this new file: the unsigned statement, the exact bytes to sign (PAE, base64), the signer, the proposed id, your log's head and a summary; needs --signer, takes no --key, appends nothing; an existing file is never overwritten"},{"option":"--signer","summary":"with --prepare: the public form of the key that will sign (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); the algorithm follows from the form"},{"option":"--complete","summary":"commit a package written by --prepare, with --signature: the bytes signed are recomputed from the package, the signature verified, every check of direct signing run again; refused, nothing appended, when your log moved since the package was prepared (stale-prepare)"},{"option":"--signature","summary":"with --complete: the signature over the package's PAE bytes in the statement encoding (ed25519: 128 lowercase hex; eip191-secp256k1: 0x + 130 lowercase hex); - reads standard input"}],"summary":"sign and commit a statement (authored with its bytes, holds, describes, retracts, or a draft)","usage":"commons publish [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--draft <file>] [--kind <kind>] [--content <file>] [--media-type <type>] [--type <type>] [--provenance <json>] [--no-holds] [--about <digest|id>] [--note <text>] [--of <id>] [--reason <text>] [--as <identity>] [--grant <id>] [--amends <id[,id…]>] [--attribution <text>] [--availability <json>] [--comparator <digest>] [--description <text>] [--extends <digest>] [--fulfills <json>] [--intended-for <resource/edition>] [--licence <text>] [--licence-texts <json>] [--pre-registration <text>] [--replication <text>] [--sampling <json>] [--shelf-life-days <integer>] [--supersedes <digest>] [--prepare <file>] [--signer <key>] [--complete <file>] [--signature <file|->] [--json]"}
{"description":"Signs a grant naming --task, --edition and --grantee with --key (or $COMMONS_KEY). Refused, with nothing signed (exit 2; exit 3 when the task or edition is not held as authorized), unless the statements this store holds resolve --edition as an authorized edition of --task whose contract this reader reads and names your key as its coordinator: any other grant has no effect. A grant counts for that edition only and whether published before or after the grantee's submission. Prints the edition's readiness with the grant added. To sign a grant anyway, use publish --draft.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--task","summary":"the task (its genesis id)"},{"option":"--edition","summary":"the task edition the grant is for (an edition id); a grant does not carry to a later edition"},{"option":"--grantee","summary":"the key granted (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>)"},{"option":"--note","summary":"an optional note carried in the grant"}],"summary":"sign a task coordinator's grant for one edition","usage":"commons publish grant [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--task <id>] [--edition <id>] [--grantee <key>] [--note <text>] [--json]"}
{"description":"A new task: --name and --contract; your key is its one controller (any one approves). A new edition: --task and --contract. Prints how the edition will be settled: by counting (its quorum), or by review when the contract has no quorum predicate, or readiness unchecked when this reader cannot read the contract.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--name","summary":"a new task's name"},{"option":"--task","summary":"an existing task (its genesis id): publish a new edition after its current one"},{"option":"--contract","summary":"the edition's contract, a JSON object: profile task-contract-v1 (or the provisional profile, read under its own name); any other profile or an unknown field leaves readiness unchecked"}],"summary":"create a task (genesis + first edition) or publish a new edition of one","usage":"commons publish task [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--name <text>] [--task <id>] [--contract <file>] [--json]"}
{"description":"<package> is a directory holding a store tree, or a tar archive of one (.tar, or gzip-compressed .tar.gz; the format is read from the file's first bytes, not its name). An archive is never extracted: its members are judged by name and bytes exactly as a directory's files are, under the scan limits of status --limits; a symlink, hard link, device or other non-regular member, and any name that is absolute or carries `..`, is refused like the same entry in a directory. With --from-checkout (a hub checkout), the top-level .git is skipped as transport metadata and reported once; without it .git is refused. Exit 0 every object admitted (possibly labelled; nothing new is not an error), 1 at least one object refused (the admitted rest is still committed, and the output says so), 2 did not start (no such directory or file, a file that is not a tar archive, a malformed option, an unusable policy), 3 nothing refused and something undecided, an archive not read to its end within the scan limits (nothing judged), or the commit failed after work started. An ambiguous archive (one member name twice, bytes after its end) is refused whole: exit 1, nothing committed.","options":[{"option":"--below-floor","summary":"a statement whose signer the reader policy's admission trust does not cover: refuse it (class reader policy, reason trust floor) or admit it labelled `below trust floor` (default label)"},{"option":"--override","summary":"a typed override: admits exactly the named objects (paths in the package) where the refusal is this reader's own policy; never an intrinsic or operational refusal"},{"option":"--from-checkout","summary":"the package is a git checkout (or an archive of one): a top-level .git entry is transport metadata, not listed to the gate and reported as skipped (never the default; a .git anywhere deeper is still refused)"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"judge the package and print the report; install nothing (no registry change; no fetch; no cache)"},{"option":"--policy","summary":"the reader policy (reader-policy-v1) to answer under (default: $COMMONS_POLICY; none when neither is set; nothing is read implicitly; format: docs/READER-POLICY.md)"}],"summary":"admit a package (a store tree in a directory or a tar archive) into this store under this reader's policy","usage":"commons pull [--below-floor <refuse|label>] [--override <name=path[,path…]>] [--from-checkout] [--dry-run] [--policy <file>] <package> [--json]"}
{"description":"Run inside a git checkout of the hub whose top level holds rc-store (a store kept elsewhere: pull it into a hub checkout first). The change is merge-base(<base>, HEAD)..HEAD: commit what you publish, then push; data files the working tree has and HEAD does not are listed as uncommitted and are not carried. Each object gets the verdict the hub's CI would give (hub check: the hub's history and hub-policy.json at the base). Then the writer's preconditions, which are not admission verdicts: each statement carried whose standing requires an anchor (an authored without provenance, a fulfills, a withheld availability, an anchored-at-capture observation) is refused, reader policy anchor required, unless an anchors line by its signer, carried or held by the hub, covers it; a pending proof covers (run anchor stamp, commit, push). A statement with standing anchor: none that nothing covers is carried with a warning naming the rule. --anchor-policy replaces the standing for every statement. A blob carried whose availability a statement declares withheld, with no serving holds (the reveal), is refused, reader policy withheld bytes: carrying it would release the bytes. An anchors line whose proof blob neither travels nor is held by the hub is carried with a warning (readers see missing-proof until it is held). Transport: only when every object is admitted and no precondition refuses, push runs git push <remote> <judged commit>:refs/heads/<branch> in your own git environment (your credentials, configuration and hooks), never forced, no tags. It sends the commit it judged, never whatever HEAD is by then. The branch is --branch, else the base's branch on the remote (origin/HEAD → main); when the base is not a branch of the remote, push exits 2 asking for --branch. To contribute through a pull request, push to a branch of your own: --branch <name> on the hub, or --remote <your fork> --base origin/main --branch <name>; then open the pull request. Pushing to the hub's default branch directly works where the hub allows it (a protected branch refuses it: exit 3, the hub's answer quoted). A refusal by git (the branch moved on, authentication, a hook, a protected branch) is exit 3 with transported false and git's message, credentials removed. Nothing is ever pushed when the judgement exits 1 or 3. Exit 0 nothing refused and nothing undecided, and (without --dry-run) the hub's branch holds the judged commit; 1 at least one object or precondition refused, nothing sent; 2 did not start (not a hub checkout, no such remote, base not known, branch not known, unusable hub-policy.json at the base); 3 nothing refused and at least one object undecided (nothing sent), or git failed or refused the push. Side effects: none in the store; no registry change; no fetch; no cache; no transport for a dry run or a judgement that is not clean, else no registry change; no fetch; no cache; one git push of the judged commit to one branch, never forced (git records the remote branch it saw in refs/remotes/<remote>/<branch>).","options":[{"option":"--remote","summary":"the git remote that is the hub (default origin)"},{"option":"--base","summary":"the hub branch the change is for, as this checkout knows it (default refs/remotes/<remote>/HEAD; exit 2 when that is not set)"},{"option":"--branch","summary":"the remote branch to update (default: the base's branch on the remote, e.g. main for origin/HEAD → origin/main); name your own branch to open a pull request from it"},{"option":"--anchor-policy","summary":"your local anchor policy for every statement carried, replacing the default by standing (bitcoin-time note §2 step 3); named in the output"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"judge and print what the push would carry; send nothing"}],"summary":"judge the commits of this hub checkout as the hub's CI would, apply the anchor and withheld-bytes preconditions, and when all is clean git push the judged commit (never forced; --dry-run sends nothing)","usage":"commons push [--remote <name>] [--base <rev>] [--branch <name>] [--anchor-policy <required|none>] [--dry-run] [--json]"}
{"description":"Every open of the store replays unfinished journal records first; repair finishes what that replay could not: a blob a committed operation names is missing (give it with --blob <file>, or commons fetch <digest>, then repair), a projection failed, or a replay failed (fix the permission or free the disk, then repair). It never signs, rewrites or deletes a statement or blob, never rolls back, and never touches quarantine/ (it prints how many items are there; inspect them with fsck). Exit 0 every named operation finished and every projection equals a cold build (an operation id no record has: nothing to repair, also 0); 1 a projection still differs from a cold build after the rebuild; 2 did not start (a malformed operation id, an operation id with --all, --blob bytes no unfinished operation names); 3 an operation could not be finished (a blob still missing, a replay still failing), or the repair failed after it started.","options":[{"option":"--all","summary":"every unfinished journal record (the default when no operation is named)"},{"option":"--blob","summary":"install these exact bytes first: they must hash to a blob an unfinished record names (the named operation's, or any with --all); hashed again on install, nothing installed on a mismatch"}],"summary":"finish committed operations the store could not finish: reinstall, re-check blobs, rebuild projections","usage":"commons repair [--all] [--blob <file>] [<operation>] [--json]"}
{"description":"A revocation cuts every line of the key outside its own history (prev, merges and their ancestors). Soft reasons (superseded, cessation_of_operation) merge every tip this store holds of your log, so nothing is cut; a tip whose branch carries a rotate_key or revoke_key outside the line you extend is refused (exit 1), because merging it would let that event outrank the revocation: use key_compromise with --last-good instead. With a forked log and no --prev, a soft revocation extends the highest tip when no branch carries a key event, and otherwise asks for --prev. key_compromise merges nothing unless you name it with --merge and needs --last-good <id> or --last-good none; on a forked log it needs --prev. Every tip is listed with what happens to it, and the lines that stop counting are named. Every identity grant naming the key is printed by id with its state (grants_not_closed): a key's own revocation closes none of them; only the identity's root does (identity cut). --dry-run signs nothing and writes nothing (with key_compromise it runs without --last-good, to list the tips). Exit 0 signed and committed (or the dry run); 1 refused after the check over your log completed (nothing signed); 2 did not start (no reason and no terminal, a missing --last-good, conflicting options, no usable key); 3 could not decide (a forked log without --prev, a line it names not held) or a failure after work started.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--reason","summary":"one of key_compromise, superseded, cessation_of_operation; no default; required when stdin is not a terminal"},{"option":"--last-good","summary":"hard reason only, required: the last line of your log you vouch for (it and its ancestors keep counting), or none"},{"option":"--merge","summary":"hard reason only: lines of your log to keep, comma-separated (merged into the revocation's history)"},{"option":"--note","summary":"a note recorded in the revocation"}],"summary":"revoke your signing key: sign a revoke_key whose history keeps what you choose","usage":"commons revoke [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--reason <reason>] [--last-good <id|none>] [--merge <id,…>] [--note <text>] [--json]"}
{"description":"A rotation retires your key and names its successor; the successor starts its own log and inherits no claims. Every identity grant naming your key is printed by id with its state (grants_not_closed): a rotation closes none of them and the successor inherits none. Like a soft revocation, it merges every tip this store holds of your log, so nothing is cut; a tip whose branch carries a rotate_key or revoke_key outside the line you extend is refused (exit 1): use revoke --reason key_compromise with --last-good instead. With a forked log and no --prev, the rotation extends the highest tip when no branch carries a key event, and otherwise asks for --prev. It is refused (exit 1) when the history it extends already holds an in-force key event (the rotation would count toward nothing) and when the successor's log here carries a key event. Every tip is listed with what happens to it. --dry-run signs nothing and writes nothing. Exit 0 signed and committed (or the dry run); 1 refused after the check over the logs completed (nothing signed); 2 did not start (no successor or both options, the successor is your own key, no usable key); 3 could not decide (a forked log without --prev, a line it names not held) or a failure after work started.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--successor","summary":"the successor key (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>); exactly one of --successor and --successor-key"},{"option":"--successor-key","summary":"the successor's key file: its signer is named (the key is never printed); exactly one of --successor and --successor-key"}],"summary":"rotate your signing key: sign a rotate_key naming its successor","usage":"commons rotate [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--successor <key>] [--successor-key <file>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Runs the workflow spec the store holds (its digest, or the id of an authored statement on it) under --exec-policy, and prints the result token, the environment record and each output; nothing is appended. An input the spec does not pin (a tag, a URL, a path) is never fetched: give its bytes with --input <name>=<file> (unpinned inputs only; a pinned input is always the held blob its hash names), recorded by digest only, never the path; the run has partial provenance (no derivation group, verify exits 4). With --publish (and --key or $COMMONS_KEY) it signs, per output: authored on a first derivation, reproduced over your own current authored of the same workflow and inputs (match on the same bytes; diverged, or the comparator's word, on different bytes; never a second authored), observed for a workflow that requires the network. Disclosures are per output (--licence out=CC-BY-4.0) and nothing is inherited: not from the workflow, its inputs or a sibling output. The provenance, environment record and capture are written from the run and cannot be supplied. A run that did not complete, or a re-run whose comparison could not finish, publishes nothing and says why. Beside each authored and observed it signs a holds (your key serves those bytes); a reproduced gets none. The statements and the output blobs commit as one unit: all or nothing.","options":[{"option":"--key","summary":"the signing key file (else $COMMONS_KEY); there is no default"},{"option":"--prev","summary":"the line of your log to extend; needed when your log has a FORK, and makes a fork when it is not a tip"},{"option":"--ts","summary":"the signer-declared time (RFC 3339 UTC); default now; more than 300 s ahead is refused"},{"option":"--dry-run","summary":"print what would be signed and committed, sign nothing, write nothing"},{"option":"--exec-policy","summary":"the reader's execution policy file (format rc-exec-policy/0, provisional); required: nothing runs without one"},{"option":"--input","summary":"the bytes of an input the spec does not pin (a tag, a URL, a path): staged under that name, recorded by digest only; refused for a pinned input"},{"option":"--publish","summary":"sign and commit what the run emits (authored, reproduced or observed per output) with the output blobs, as one unit"},{"option":"--request-log","summary":"an acquisition's request log output (rc-request-log/1): its referent's format, count and endpoint are read from it"},{"option":"--of","summary":"the output re-runs this statement of yours (an authored under the same provenance); needed only when the run has several outputs and their bytes changed"},{"option":"--media-type","summary":"the output's content.media_type"},{"option":"--attribution","summary":"per output: authored disclosure attribution"},{"option":"--availability","summary":"per output: authored disclosure availability"},{"option":"--comparator","summary":"per output: authored disclosure comparator"},{"option":"--description","summary":"per output: authored disclosure description"},{"option":"--extends","summary":"per output: authored disclosure extends"},{"option":"--fulfills","summary":"per output: authored disclosure fulfills"},{"option":"--intended-for","summary":"per output: run the edition's rules on the bytes before they are pinned (your own policy, chosen by naming the edition); fail refuses, ok or unchecked is recorded"},{"option":"--licence","summary":"per output: authored disclosure licence"},{"option":"--licence-texts","summary":"per output: authored disclosure licence_texts"},{"option":"--pre-registration","summary":"per output: authored disclosure pre_registration"},{"option":"--replication","summary":"per output: authored disclosure replication"},{"option":"--sampling","summary":"per output: authored disclosure sampling"},{"option":"--shelf-life-days","summary":"per output: authored disclosure shelf_life_days"},{"option":"--supersedes","summary":"per output: authored disclosure supersedes"}],"summary":"run a held workflow under your execution policy; --publish signs what it emits","usage":"commons run [--key <file>] [--prev <id>] [--ts <time>] [--dry-run] [--exec-policy <file>] [--input <name=file>]… [--publish] [--request-log <output>] [--of <output=id>]… [--media-type <output=type>]… [--attribution <output=text>]… [--availability <output=json>]… [--comparator <output=digest>]… [--description <output=text>]… [--extends <output=digest>]… [--fulfills <output=json>]… [--intended-for <output=resource/edition>]… [--licence <output=text>]… [--licence-texts <output=json>]… [--pre-registration <output=text>]… [--replication <output=text>]… [--sampling <output=json>]… [--shelf-life-days <output=integer>]… [--supersedes <output=digest>]… <workflow> [--json]"}
{"description":"A digest shows the blob: authorship and every claimant, labels, whether the bytes are held here, the grade with the full disclosure set beside it, derivation groups, and every other statement about it. An id shows the statement. A resource's id is its genesis statement's id: show prints the genesis and the resource view beside it (controllers and rule, the current edition and the chain behind it by hash, every edition with its approvals, any FORK, the current edition's members and the receiver's check of a completeness claim). An edition id, or the statement id of one of its approvals, shows the resource pinned at that edition. An observation's statement shows its corroboration status. A statement on a fork of its signer's log shows the fork (FORK with its tips, or merged with the clearing line); it never changes whether the statement counts. An identity statement shows its actor (identity, grant, eligible / denied / unknown, the reasons) with freshness not_established: relative to held evidence; no claim that no cut exists. A key (ed25519:<64 hex> or 0x<40 hex>) shows its two axes side by side: bare_key, its own rotate_key and revoke_key and what each leaves counting, and grants, every grant naming the key by id with its state (open, cut at last_good, cut null, unknown, denied); a key's own key event closes no grant.","options":[{"option":"--policy","summary":"the reader policy (reader-policy-v1) to answer under (default: $COMMONS_POLICY; none when neither is set; nothing is read implicitly; format: docs/READER-POLICY.md)"}],"summary":"show a blob (authorship, grade, every disclosure), a statement, a resource, or a key","usage":"commons show [--policy <file>] <digest|id> [--json]"}
{"description":"Every open of the store replays unfinished journal records first; status reports what that replay did and every record still unfinished, with the error and the next step when a replay failed. An unfinished operation is finished by commons repair (or by the next open) once its cause is fixed: give a missing blob with repair --blob <file> or fetch it, free the disk, fix a permission. The journal record of an operation is deleted once it is finished; durability.history declares the local operation history (commons history lists it), which status never reads: what is unfinished is decided from journal records alone.","options":[{"option":"--limits","summary":"also print every limit this tool applies: name, value, where the value comes from and what it bounds"},{"option":"--policy","summary":"the reader policy (reader-policy-v1) to answer under (default: $COMMONS_POLICY; none when neither is set; nothing is read implicitly; format: docs/READER-POLICY.md)"}],"summary":"store recovery state, unfinished operations and the durability declaration","usage":"commons status [--limits] [--policy <file>] [--json]"}
{"description":"Statement-only by default: verify reads the statements this store holds and runs nothing. With --rerun (a blob only) and --exec-policy <file>, the reader re-runs the derivation under its own execution policy and the re-run's token replaces the derivation cause; without a policy the re-run is unchecked and nothing runs. Exit 0 checked and passed, 1 a completed comparison disagreed, 3 could not check, 4 nothing to re-run, 5 environment mismatch.","options":[{"option":"--rerun","summary":"re-run the blob's derivation under --exec-policy and compare it with the pin (a blob only); never the default"},{"option":"--exec-policy","summary":"the reader's execution policy file (format rc-exec-policy/0, provisional); only applies alongside --rerun"},{"option":"--policy","summary":"the reader policy (reader-policy-v1) to answer under (default: $COMMONS_POLICY; none when neither is set; nothing is read implicitly; format: docs/READER-POLICY.md)"}],"summary":"check a blob or a statement from the statements held; exit per the exit table","usage":"commons verify [--rerun] [--exec-policy <file>] [--policy <file>] <digest|id> [--json]"}
